                <pb facs="0259" n="159" sameAs="frus:frus1920v03/pg159"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">812.00/24416: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Mexicali</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Boyle</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Calexico, Calif.</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-07-31"><hi rendition="#italic">July 31,
                        1920</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received 9 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>Your July 30th, 8 p.m. After conference with military commander at Calexico and
                    his concurrence in my view that presence of American aviators in service of
                    Cantu, no matter what the status claimed, in the present crisis could be
                    construed in no other light than that of military service, but acting on my own
                    authority, I have requested the permit agent at Calexico to take up the border
                    cards of aviator[s] Payne and Goree. I have also requested him to take up the
                    border permit of Major Parr, a British subject just arrived from the United
                    States and employed by Cantu to direct his aviation service. In event expected
                    invasion does not eventuate will recommend return of these border cards.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Boyle</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
